-- title:
--   The uniform geometric decay rate for Walsh norms
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Fix $m\ge1$ and a threshold $n_0\in\mathbb N$.
--
--   1. **Uniform decay rate.** For $N\in\mathbb N$,
--   $$
--   \rho_{m,n_0}(N)=\Bigl(\tfrac12\Bigr)^{\lfloor (N-n_0)/(2m)\rfloor},
--   $$
--   with truncated subtraction, so $\rho_{m,n_0}(N)=1$ for $N\le n_0$. This is the rate obtained when a nonincreasing sequence halves every $2m$ steps after $n_0$. It tends to $0$ as $N\to\infty$, and it does not depend on the operator, which makes the Walsh-norm decay of finite-rank operators uniform.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/Cotype/UniformDecay.lean

